---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Abstraction</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 2.1 &ndash; 2.7. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> Defining and Calling</h2>

          <p>
            Give a block of code a name, then run
            it by that name. Defining and calling are separate acts, and
            repeated code is the signal that a procedure is
            missing.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Function</dt>
            <dd>A named block of code that runs when you call it.</dd>
            <dt>Define</dt>
            <dd>Write the function and give it a name. Nothing runs yet.</dd>
            <dt>Call</dt>
            <dd>Run it, using its name followed by <code>()</code>.</dd>
            <dt>Procedure</dt>
            <dd>The exam's word for the same thing. It is what the reference sheet uses.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> Parameters</h2>

          <p>
            A parameter is a blank left in the definition.
            Arguments fill those blanks in order, by position &mdash; the names
            you use at the call site are irrelevant.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Parameter</dt>
            <dd>The named blank in the definition.</dd>
            <dt>Argument</dt>
            <dd>The actual value passed in when you call.</dd>
            <dt>Positional</dt>
            <dd>Arguments fill parameters left to right, by position only.</dd>
            <dt>undefined</dt>
            <dd>What a parameter holds when no argument was supplied.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> Return Values</h2>

          <p>
            Printing shows a value to a person; returning
            hands it back to the code that asked. Only one of those can be used
            in another calculation.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Return value</dt>
            <dd>The value a procedure hands back to its caller.</dd>
            <dt>return</dt>
            <dd>Sends a value back <i>and</i> exits the procedure immediately.</dd>
            <dt>undefined</dt>
            <dd>What a procedure hands back when it has no <code>return</code>.</dd>
            <dt>Composition</dt>
            <dd>Using one procedure's return value as another's argument.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> p5 Lab 7: Parameters You Can See</h2>

          <p>
            One procedure, many
            pictures. This is also where the Create Task's central requirement
            first appears: a procedure of your own whose parameter changes what
            it produces.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Affects functionality</dt>
            <dd>Changing the argument changes what the procedure produces.</dd>
            <dt>Student-developed procedure</dt>
            <dd>One you wrote. <code>setup()</code> and <code>draw()</code> do not count.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">5.</span> Multiple Parameters, Scope, and Abstraction</h2>

          <p>
            Where a name
            lives decides where it means anything. Abstraction is the payoff
            &mdash; once the detail is behind a name, you get to stop thinking
            about it.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>The region of a program where a name means something.</dd>
            <dt>Local variable</dt>
            <dd>Declared inside a procedure; gone when the call ends.</dd>
            <dt>Shadowing</dt>
            <dd>An inner name hiding an outer one that shares its spelling.</dd>
            <dt>Abstraction</dt>
            <dd>Hiding detail behind a name so you can stop thinking about it.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">6.</span> Refactoring</h2>

          <p>
            Refactoring changes how code is written without
            changing what it does &mdash; which is only a claim you can make if
            you tested it before and after.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Refactoring</dt>
            <dd>Changing how code is written without changing what it does.</dd>
            <dt>Extract a procedure</dt>
            <dd>Move repeated code into a named procedure and call it.</dd>
            <dt>Regression</dt>
            <dd>A change that was supposed to be safe and was not.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">7.</span> p5 Lab 8: Composing a Scene</h2>

          <p>
            Procedures become building
            blocks, and the mouse replaces the prompt. Note carefully what does
            not count as code you developed.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Event handler</dt>
            <dd>A procedure the system calls when something happens.</dd>
            <dt>mouseX / mouseY</dt>
            <dd>Where the pointer was when the event fired.</dd>
            <dt>Student-developed procedure</dt>
            <dd>One you wrote and call yourself. Not an event handler.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
